Unit size / aggregation (Civ6-style formations)
================================================

Context (2026-09)
-----------------
World unit totals on save timelines approach the u16 entity ceiling (~61k by late game
in a 100-seat run). Playing with >50k discrete units is not enjoyable to manage, so the
first capacity fix should be gameplay aggregation, not widening indices to 32-bit or
splitting storage by player.

Rejected / deferred for later
-----------------------------
1) Widen unit indices to 4 bytes — clean but raises memory/I/O for every unit touch;
   better if we truly need huge simultaneous field armies.
2) Separate idle/garrison arrays from adventuring u16 pool — good complement if most
   mass is city/fort parked units; does not replace aggregation for field stacks.
3) Per-player unit arrays — scales capacity per seat and helps per-player iteration
   (player id already on units), but forces composite ids and reworks tile occupancy;
   larger migration than aggregation. Revisit after formations.

Chosen direction: unit size field + MAX_UNIT_SIZE
-------------------------------------------------
- Add a small bitfield on the runtime unit (about 4 bits) holding formation size
  (consolidated count / formation tier; prefer count-style power scaling unless we
  decide otherwise).
- New civ/player runtime stat: MAX_UNIT_SIZE. Tech tree (and possibly wonders/traits)
  grants +1 effects that raise this cap. Usable size must never exceed the player's
  current MAX_UNIT_SIZE.
- AI heavily biased to always aggregate eligible stacks (same tile, same catalog unit
  or type — rules TBD). Prefer reinforcing/merging over spawning new entities when
  under the size cap.
- UnitRosterMng / production still select a catalog unit; aggregation is a post-build
  / on-tile policy.

Open design points (lock before coding)
---------------------------------------
- Merge eligibility: same unit row only? same type? domain? tile-only / city / fort?
- Overflow when a+b > MAX: partial merge? refuse?
- Split / disband for settlers, workers, transport; XP, damage, upgrades vs size.
- Which types are excluded from aggregation (scouts, settlers, workers)?
- Combat math: strength scales with size how?

Related lever
-------------
City-bound garrison / third support type is tracked in notes.unit_support; it cuts
static home-guard entity spam and pairs with aggregation for field armies.
